00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.230
Apigee has native policies
available within the UI that

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:33.500
will make the API fulfillment
process even more turnkey.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.660
These policies can pars
DialogFlow requests,

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.210
set DialogFlow responses,
and validate parameters

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.120
captured by DialogFlow.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.490
I built a simple flow
in DialogFlow CX that

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.200
allows someone to rent a car.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.990
The user is asked when and where
they want to pick up the car.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.970
They pick a specific car,
confirm some details,

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.350
and the rental
reservation is booked.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.350
Let's run in the simulator
and see how it works.

00:01:58.775 --> 00:01:59.275
Hello.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:03.640
I'd like to rent a car.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.710
San Jose, California.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:10.210
No.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:17.310
June 13th, 3:00 PM.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.900
June 15th, 9:00 AM.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:22.800
Yes, that sounds good.

00:02:25.547 --> 00:02:26.630
Let's go with the Charger.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:30.120
Yes.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:33.230
Visa.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:45.170
412341234.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:45.920
John Smith.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:56.940
2930 Pearl Street,
Boulder, Colorado 80301.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:02.400
80301.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.650
It was easy for me to rent
a car with this agent.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.808
The conversation was natural
and the agent was responsive.

00:03:07.808 --> 00:03:10.350
You may have noticed the list
of parameters in the simulator.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.240
DialogFlow allows
you to store pieces

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.620
of information and parameters.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.412
Some of these come from
what the user says,

00:03:15.412 --> 00:03:17.970
and others are retrieved
from backend services.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.760
A big part of creating a good
conversational application

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.740
is integrating with
backend systems.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.810
DialogFlow lets you
specify a webhook

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.030
to connect with backend systems.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.340
For example, I used a
webhook in the list vehicle

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.240
step of my flow.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.820
When the conversation
reaches this step,

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.160
DialogFlow will make a call
to the backend system that

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.950
has car inventory information.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.470
In this demo, I used Apigee
to build a set of APIs

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.070
for my agent.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.950
Apigee lets you quickly
build and manage APIs.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:51.327
Apigee has two new policies just
for working with DialogFlow.

00:03:51.327 --> 00:03:53.160
If we look at the step
where the agent lists

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.900
which cars are
available, we can see

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:59.040
that it uses the Get Vehicles
webhook in the Search Cars tag.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.050
Let's look at the
definition for that webhook.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.900
For the webhook URL, I
provided the URL for the API

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.430
on managing in Apigee.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.530
I also supplied a
client key from Apigee

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.110
that will be passed
as an HTTP header.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.940
This client key
identifies the application

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.040
and lets Apigee control
access to the APIs it manages.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.079
Next, let's look at how
this API works in Apigee.

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:21.269
In Apigee, I
created an API proxy

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:23.370
to handle requests
from DialogFlow.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.590
I've configured a set of
Apigee policies over here,

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.650
and I have a series of flows
that handle specific requests.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:32.160
The proxy endpoint pre flow
is called for our request.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.720
I also have four
conditional flows

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.040
that will be called
depending on which

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.290
tag is specified in the
webhook fulfillment setting.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.370
In the pre flow, there
are three policies.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.400
The first checks the API key
that is passed in the header.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:47.840
If the key is
missing or invalid,

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.460
an unauthorized
response is sent.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.400
Otherwise, the flow continues.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.140
Next, I remove the
client key header,

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.570
since it's no longer needed.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:00.032
The third policy is an instance
of parsed DialogFlow request,

00:05:00.032 --> 00:05:01.490
one of the new
policies for working

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:02.990
with DialogFlow in Apigee.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.870
Messages sent from DialogFlow
have a JSON payload

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:09.110
that contains the intent,
session ID parameters, and more

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:10.820
that are sent with
the webhook request.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:12.795
Parsed DialogFlow request
takes his information

00:05:12.795 --> 00:05:14.420
and turns it into
variables that can be

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:16.760
referenced in this Apigee flow.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:18.640
Let's turn on the trace
and see how it works.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.810
If we look at the trace,
we can see all the details

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:28.920
of what happened in Apigee
when the webhook was called.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.360
The request enters here.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.280
A number of policies are called.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.347
We'll notice here in the
parse DialogFlow request

00:05:34.347 --> 00:05:36.180
that a whole bunch of
variables got created.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.470
And those were
based on parameters

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.450
that were present in our
session in DialogFlow.

00:05:44.168 --> 00:05:46.210
All of these will be
available at the Apigee flow

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:47.793
and can be used in
different policies.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.070
We set a condition to
follow a conditional flow

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:57.620
based on the value of
the fulfillment tag.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:00.470
In this case, the fulfillment
tag was equal to Search Cars,

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:02.120
and this was a post request.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:03.710
So that particular
flow was followed

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:05.345
and these steps were executed.

00:06:08.972 --> 00:06:10.430
Let's look at the
flow that manages

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:11.780
the booking of a reservation.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:13.790
This flow calls out
to a Cloud function.

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:15.790
The Cloud function
requires authentication.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:17.540
The first two policies
in here make a call

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.652
to the Google Cloud
Identity Management system.

00:06:19.652 --> 00:06:22.235
They send some credentials that
I stored in Apigee's key value

00:06:22.235 --> 00:06:23.158
map.

00:06:23.158 --> 00:06:24.950
Those credentials are
exchanged for a token

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.708
that I can pass in a header to
the call to my Cloud function.

00:06:28.708 --> 00:06:30.750
The next policy is the
call to the Cloud function

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.360
that will make a reservation.

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.070
I send the token in
several parameters

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.050
that were sent from DialogFlow.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.180
The car description, the
start date, the end date,

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:39.620
and the location.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.840
Apigee then makes a call
to the reservation service

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.790
and reserves a car.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:46.320
The response is
stored in a variable

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.080
called Reserve Results.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.452
The next policy is an
extract variable policy.

00:06:51.452 --> 00:06:53.160
And it's used to
retrieve the reservation

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.610
ID and the reservation total
from the response from the call

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.340
to the reservation system.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.980
Next I use a set
DialogFlow response policy

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.960
to send the reservation
ID and total back

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.030
to DialogFlow's parameters.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:08.790
DialogFlow uses that information
to confirm the reservation

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:11.250
with the customer.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:14.190
Using Apigee to manage the
APIs needed by DialogFlow

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:15.780
allows a conversation
designer who's

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.880
building things in
DialogFlow to focus

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:19.800
on providing an excellent
user experience,

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.258
rather than worrying about how
to integrate with Enterprise

00:07:22.258 --> 00:07:22.830
systems.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:24.960
It allows an API
developer to quickly build

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.882
a set of experienced APIs
to support this application.

00:07:27.882 --> 00:07:29.340
They can hide away
the complexities

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:30.870
in the backend services.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.400
This won't be the
last API we need

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.775
to build for this project or
something else that requires

00:07:34.775 --> 00:07:35.937
access to backend services.

00:07:35.937 --> 00:07:37.770
But establishing a
standard pattern for this

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.230
will save us a lot of
time in the long run.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.290
Apigee, and the two new policies
specifically for DialogFlow

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:44.833
make this easy to do.

00:07:44.833 --> 00:07:46.500
Now it's your turn
to try it out and get

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.833
started by following the links
in the description below.

